  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in through mid week.\n\nYesterday's cold front will slow and dissipate over the\nCarolinas today as high pressure builds in from the Ohio River\nValley. Prior to the arrival of high pressure, some lingering\nmoisture and very weak ascent may cause a few pop up showers \nand thunderstorms along the I-64 corridor in Virginia over to \nsouthern Maryland this afternoon into early this evening. Weak \nforcing and modest CAPE (with a notable capping inversion just \nabove 700 hPa and poor mid-level lapse rates) should keep storm\ncoverage and intensity low.\n\nHigh pressure will build across the Mid-Atlantic Wednesday with\ndry conditions expected. A cold front will approach from the\nGreat Lakes bringing an increasing chance of showers and perhaps\na thunderstorm by late Wednesday night.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily chances for showers and thunderstorms return\nThursday, then continue Friday through Sunday.\n\nAlthough timing remains a bit uncertain, a cold front looks to\nenter the area Thursday morning, then slowly cross through\nThursday night. Questions regarding early day precip and cloud\ncover lend to uncertainty in how much instability builds\nThursday afternoon, though a few stronger storms are certainly\npossible given increased flow aloft and lift along the front.\n\nModel guidance is in relatively good agreement that the longwave \npattern across the CONUS will amplify significantly heading into \nnext weekend as unseasonably deep troughing digs across the Great \nLakes and Ohio Valley. While there is some spread with respect to \njust how deep this trough will be and its exact east/west placement, \nnearly all solutions place us downstream of the trough, which will \nkeep us in southwesterly flow aloft and keep the pattern active. \nDaily chances for showers and thunderstorms are expected within the \nzone of large scale ascent downstream of the longwave trough \nsharpening to our west. Depending on how much instability develops, \nsome of these storms could potentially be on the stronger side. \nTemperatures are forecast to run slightly below normal, with highs \nin the upper 70s and low 80s, and overnight lows in the 60s.\n\nMost solutions have the longwave trough making some eastward \nprogress by Sunday night into Monday, which should result in a cold \nfront passing through. Continued below normal temperatures and drier \nconditions are forecast behind the front for Monday.",
